# Scripted RCPT replies — the open.relay peer doubles as the simulated
# internet's MISBEHAVING neighbor. A recipient of the form
#
#   reply-<smtp code>-<class>-<subject>-<detail>[-<anything>]@open.relay
#
# is answered at RCPT with exactly that SMTP code and RFC 3463 enhanced
# status code, e.g.
#
#   reply-550-5-7-1-x9k2@open.relay   ->  550 5.7.1 scripted reject ...
#   reply-450-4-2-1-x9k2@open.relay   ->  450 4.2.1 scripted tempfail ...
#
# so stories can provoke any refusal the real internet produces (policy
# rejects, quota tempfails, ...) as genuine wire replies instead of faking
# DSNs. The trailing free segment keeps addresses unique per test
# (parallel-safe convention). The SMTP class and enhanced class are tied
# by the patterns (4xx <-> 4.x.x, 5xx <-> 5.x.x) because postfix refuses
# mismatched pairs.
#
# The table is installed on every peer but inert off open.relay: the
# pattern requires the domain, and no persona uses the reply- prefix.
/^reply-(4\d\d)-4-(\d{1,3})-(\d{1,3})(-[^@]*)?@open\.relay$/ $1 4.$2.$3 scripted tempfail from open.relay
/^reply-(5\d\d)-5-(\d{1,3})-(\d{1,3})(-[^@]*)?@open\.relay$/ $1 5.$2.$3 scripted reject from open.relay
